// Every boundary is one funnel (#1191 addendum, owner, 2026-10-19). Each capability that crosses a boundary
// has exactly one gateway; adapters fan out behind it. Callers never know which adapter served them, and
// moving work between Cloudflare and boxd never moves a funnel. A second path to a capability is a bug.
views {

  view funnels {
    title 'Overview/Decisions/Funnels'
    description '''
      Which one gateway does each capability cross? Six funnels: Funnel (clients), Command gateway
      (`Computer.exec` / `attach`), Files (`Computer.files` / Drive), Model gateway, Connections gateway
      (Executor plus egress) and Port gateway (`Computer.port`). Callers sit above, adapters below, and the
      kernel on Cloudflare and a boxd VM sit behind the same three Computer funnels. Click a funnel for its
      callers and adapters. Decided 2026-10-19 (#1191).
    '''

    include
      signalbox.web with { title 'Clients'; description 'web · desktop · mobile · CLI · API' },
      signalbox.harness with { title 'Agents'; description 'model tool calls · hooks' },
      signalbox.automations,
      signalbox.funnel with { navigateTo funnel_clients },
      signalbox.computer.commandGateway with { navigateTo funnel_command },
      signalbox.computer.filesGateway with { navigateTo funnel_files },
      signalbox.computer.portGateway with { navigateTo funnel_port },
      signalbox.gateway.modelGateway with { title 'Model gateway'; navigateTo funnel_model },
      signalbox.gateway.toolGateway with { title 'Connections gateway'; description 'Executor + egress'; navigateTo funnel_connections },
      signalbox.computer.childIsolates with { title 'Kernel program'; description 'Cloudflare · child Dynamic Worker' },
      signalbox.computer.execDaemon with { title 'boxd VM'; description 'exec daemon · node-pty' }

    style signalbox.funnel, signalbox.computer.commandGateway, signalbox.computer.filesGateway, signalbox.computer.portGateway, signalbox.gateway.modelGateway, signalbox.gateway.toolGateway {
      color green
    }
    style signalbox.computer.childIsolates, signalbox.computer.execDaemon { color muted }
    rank source { signalbox.web, signalbox.harness, signalbox.automations }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  // ───────────────────────────── One drill-down per funnel ─────────────────────────────

  view funnel_clients {
    title 'Overview/Decisions/Funnels/Funnel'
    description '''
      Who reaches Signalbox through Funnel, and what is behind it? Every client (web, desktop, mobile, CLI,
      extension) speaks one typed RPC to Funnel; Funnel decodes, calls one module and maps errors. The terminal,
      files and port URLs go on into Computer's own funnels.
    '''
    include
      signalbox.web,
      signalbox.desktop,
      signalbox.mobile,
      signalbox.cli,
      signalbox.browserExtension,
      signalbox.funnel,
      signalbox.operations,
      signalbox.identity,
      signalbox.computer.commandGateway,
      signalbox.computer.filesGateway,
      signalbox.computer.portGateway
    style signalbox.funnel { color green }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  view funnel_command {
    title 'Overview/Decisions/Funnels/Command gateway'
    description '''
      Who runs commands, and where do they run? Every caller goes through `Computer.exec` / `attach`: the
      model's tool calls and hooks, the person's terminal and API agents (through Funnel), automations and stdio
      MCP servers. One routing table decides from argv and evidence, never from the caller, and every command
      runs under a budget in a child Dynamic Worker or on a boxd VM. Decided 2026-10-19 (#1071, #1255).
    '''
    include
      signalbox.funnel with { description 'terminal · API agents' },
      signalbox.harness with { description 'model tool calls · hooks' },
      signalbox.automations,
      signalbox.connections with { description 'stdio MCP servers' },
      signalbox.computer.commandGateway,
      signalbox.computer.escalation,
      signalbox.computer.budgets,
      signalbox.computer.childIsolates with { title 'Kernel program' },
      signalbox.computer.execDaemon with { title 'boxd VM' }
    exclude signalbox.automations -> signalbox.connections
    style signalbox.computer.commandGateway { color green }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  view funnel_files {
    title 'Overview/Decisions/Funnels/Files'
    description '''
      Who reads and writes files, and where do the bytes live? The harness, the terminal, the renderer and
      laptops all use `Computer.files`. Behind it: the live FS in the kernel, the thread's Artifacts fork and
      R2 through Drive, and a VM disk that is only ever a cache.
    '''
    include
      signalbox.harness,
      signalbox.funnel with { description 'terminal · renderer · laptops' },
      signalbox.computer.filesGateway,
      signalbox.computer.backend.kernelFs with { title 'Live FS'; description 'kernel · inode store' },
      signalbox.drive with { description 'Artifacts fork · R2' },
      signalbox.computer.execDaemon with { title 'VM disk'; description 'cache only' }
    style signalbox.computer.filesGateway { color green }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  view funnel_model {
    title 'Overview/Decisions/Funnels/Model gateway'
    description '''
      Who calls a model, and who answers? The harness in the isolate and a person's local Claude Code or Codex
      both use the one model gateway. Behind it the folder's pool picks an account, then the provider answers.
    '''
    include
      signalbox.harness,
      laptopCli,
      signalbox.gateway.modelGateway,
      signalbox.models with { description 'pools, then providers' },
      modelProviders.anthropic,
      modelProviders.openai,
      modelProviders.openrouter
    style signalbox.gateway.modelGateway { color green }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  view funnel_connections {
    title 'Overview/Decisions/Funnels/Connections gateway'
    description '''
      Who calls an outside service, and from where? MCP calls enter at ToolGateway (Executor); every outbound
      request from the isolate or a VM enters at egress. Both end on Cloudflare: the gateway fills the
      credential, makes the call and scrubs the response, wherever the command ran. A VM's EgressProxy is only
      a bridge: it terminates TLS for bound hosts and forwards over HTTPS; unbound allowlisted hosts splice.
    '''
    include
      signalbox.harness,
      mcpClient,
      signalbox.computer.childIsolates with { title 'Kernel program' },
      signalbox.computer.execDaemon with { title 'boxd VM' },
      signalbox.gateway.toolGateway with { title 'ToolGateway · Executor' },
      signalbox.gateway.egress,
      signalbox.gateway.egress.inProcess,
      signalbox.gateway.egress.egressProxy with { title 'EgressProxy bridge' },
      signalbox.gateway.egress.fill,
      signalbox.connections,
      services.boundHost,
      services.toolService
    style signalbox.gateway.toolGateway, signalbox.gateway.egress.fill { color green }
    style signalbox.gateway.egress { color muted; opacity 10% }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }

  view funnel_port {
    title 'Overview/Decisions/Funnels/Port gateway'
    description '''
      Who opens a port on the thread's computer, and what serves it? The person through Funnel's authenticated
      URL, and the renderer, use `Computer.port`. Behind it a kernel program or a VM's tunnel answers, and the
      URL never changes when the server moves.
    '''
    include
      personA,
      signalbox.web,
      signalbox.funnel with { title 'Funnel · port URLs'; description 'one authenticated URL per port' },
      signalbox.browser with { description 'renderer' },
      signalbox.computer.portGateway,
      signalbox.computer.childIsolates with { title 'Kernel program' },
      signalbox.computer.execDaemon with { title "VM's tunnel" }
    exclude signalbox.browser -> signalbox.funnel
    style signalbox.computer.portGateway { color green }
    autoLayout TopBottom
  }
}
